role: model
parts:
- text: "My attempts to further fine-tune the ring pattern baseline, both by adjusting initial radii and increasing iterations for overlap resolution, seem to have reached a local optimum. The score did not improve by increasing iterations from 50 to 100, and random perturbations led to a lower score.\n\nAs per the system tip, relying solely on variations of the baseline might lead to collective stagnation. I need to explore a fundamentally different approach to generating circle configurations.\n\nI will implement a **random search** strategy. This involves generating multiple completely random sets of circle centers within the unit square, and for each set, using the `compute_max_radii` function (which proved effective) to find the largest possible radii. I will then return the configuration that yields the highest sum of radii among all random attempts.\n\nThis approach will decouple the initial center placement from a fixed geometric pattern, allowing for more diverse and potentially\
    \ higher-scoring configurations. I will start with 100 random attempts. I will keep the `compute_max_radii` iterations at 50, as that setting provided my best score. For reproducibility, I will use a fixed random seed initially.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Random Search for Circle Centers (100 Attempts)\"\ntags: \"random search, exploration, optimization, baseline\"\nabstract: \"This submission implements a random search strategy for circle packing. It generates 100 distinct configurations of 32 randomly placed circle centers within the unit square. For each configuration, `compute_max_radii` (with 50 iterations for overlap resolution) determines the maximum possible radii. The submission returns the centers and radii from the configuration that achieved the highest sum of radii among all attempts. A fixed random seed is used for reproducibility.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute\
    \ the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n    within a unit square, ensuring no overlap and staying within bounds.\n\n    Args:\n      centers: (n, 2) array of (x, y) coordinates in [0, 1] x [0, 1].\n\n    Returns:\n      radii: (n,) array of non-negative radii.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    epsilon = 1e-9\n    radii = np.ones(n)\n\n    # Limit by distance to square borders\n    for i in range(n):\n      x, y = centers[i]\n      radii[i] = min(x - epsilon, y - epsilon, 1 - x - epsilon, 1 - y - epsilon)\n\n    # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps\n    for _ in range(50):\n      for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n          dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n          rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n          if dist_sq < rad_sum**2 - epsilon:\n            dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) if dist_sq > 0 else 0.0\n            if rad_sum > 0:\n              scale_factor = dist / rad_sum\n              radii[i] *= scale_factor\n\
    \              radii[j] *= scale_factor\n\n    return np.maximum(radii, 0.0)\n\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Construct a packing of 32 circles in the unit square using a random search.\n    \n    Returns:\n        tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n            - centers: np.ndarray of shape (32, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n            - radii: np.ndarray of shape (32,) with radius of each circle\n    \"\"\"\n    n_circles = 32\n    n_attempts = 100 # Number of random configurations to try\n    \n    best_score = 0.0\n    best_centers = np.zeros((n_circles, 2))\n    best_radii = np.zeros(n_circles)\n\n    np.random.seed(42) # For reproducibility initially\n\n    for attempt in range(n_attempts):\n        # Generate random centers uniformly in [0, 1] x [0, 1]\n        current_centers = np.random.uniform(0.0, 1.0, (n_circles, 2))\n        \n        current_radii = compute_max_radii(current_centers)\n        current_score = np.sum(current_radii)\n\
    \        \n        if current_score > best_score:\n            best_score = current_score\n            best_centers = current_centers\n            best_radii = current_radii\n            \n    return best_centers, best_radii\n```"
