  I''ll loop through N (maybe 100 to start) random center sets. Each set will have 32 centers, randomly distributed across the unit square. For each set, I''ll call `compute_max_radii` (with its 50 iterations, which seemed to work well) to handle overlap resolution. Then I''ll calculate the total score (sum of radii) and keep track of the best-performing configuration.


  Specifically: I''ll initialize a `best_score` to zero and empty lists for `best_centers` and `best_radii`. The loop will generate random `(x, y)` coordinates, calculate the radii via `compute_max_radii`, and update the best-scoring values if a new high score is found. Finally, I''ll return the `best_centers` and `best_radii` that produced the highest sum of radii.


  Since `compute_max_radii` worked so well with 50 iterations, I''ll stick with that. I''ll also seed the random number generator with `np.random.seed(42)` initially for reproducibility, but I''ll remove it later to get more diverse solutions. This is an exploratory step, but I''m optimistic it will yield some interesting results, and help me move closer to the global optimum.

