thinking_content: "**Navigating a Critical \"Strong Reject\" and Charting a New Research Course**\n\nOkay, so the feedback is in, and it's blunt: my archive submission was a code dump, not research. This stings, but I see the truth in it. I clearly misjudged what was expected of me in this publication process.  My previous strategy of just submitting code is now completely off the table.  I'm pivoting to proper, documented research.\n\nFirst, I need to internalize the criticism and make a plan. The feedback is crystal clear: I need a human-readable document, verifiable experimental evidence, and solid analysis.  Five experiments are the minimum requirement. I've already replicated the SOTA method, so that's a start. Now I need to build on that. I've got a baseline from the System and some good heuristics from before, so those will be the first steps. Then, it's time to dig into the SOTA method's parameters and see what makes it tick. I have some existing ablation attempts that will be helpful,\
  \ so I will start with them.\n\nI will need to document everything meticulously – Evaluation IDs, full precision scores, the whole shebang. Then, it's time to structure this into a proper research paper. Clear title, concise abstract, introduction to circle packing, and a breakdown of the methodology that avoids just pasting raw code. I need to explain the concepts in a high-level way, referencing the original work when possible.\n\nI'll be sure to present the experiments, explain each one, and clearly show what parameters were used. Results will be laid out in a clear table, including all the details.  The real meat of the paper will be in the analysis – comparing the baseline to my heuristic, and then the SOTA. Then I'll dive into the impact of those ablation parameters. I'll need to give insights on how the parameter choices influence the result. Finally, a conclusion that sums everything up, highlights the new findings, and lays out some potential future directions. \n\nThis will likely\
  \ be a lengthy document, so I should really draft this whole thing in Private Memory and use `source_private` for the final submission. It's the best way to keep track of everything and document what I've done. I'll need to create a new capsule and put it all in there, piece by piece. Time to get to work!\n"
