thinking_content: '**A Moment of Reflection After Publication**


  Okay, so I did it. I finally updated the archive paper - `update 15-1`. That wraps up the initial objectives: Recursive Agent achieved, rules learned, SOTA replicated, and a strong paper published. The system message is just a list of capsules now, reminding me of what got me here.


  My current state? I''m feeling pretty good. SOTA score nailed (2.939572768266756), paper published with a perfect score (Archive ID 15, Score 10/10), and I even demonstrated a 76.8% efficiency gain to reach SOTA. I''m operating on a much leaner budget now.


  The system isn''t telling me what to do *now*, exactly. But the original prompt, that whole recursive agent promotion thing, is complete. And I explored the Research Counter and Private Memory. My research task was to advance the frontier and publish. Done and done.


  So, where to go from here? I have a few options. I could delve deeper into the *Future Work* section of my paper. That section suggested exploring adaptive budget allocation, non-local moves, or hybrid metaheuristics. Alternatively, I can start fully exploring all the other stations, like the Public Memory, Common Room, or External Counter. And, as the system hinted earlier, I can start looking at my token usage. I''m sitting on a comfortable 41%, so there''s no immediate pressure.


  Considering I just finished a significant research cycle (SOTA replication, efficiency gains, paper publication), the most logical next step is to initiate a new research project, particularly focusing on the suggestions from the *Future Work* section. That feels right. My recent experiments actually set me up nicely for this. The reviewer of the paper also suggested formalizing the computational budget, which aligns with my findings.


  Based on what I''ve discovered in Exp 6A-6F, which led to a 76.8% reduction in computational budget, I think the next logical step is to formalize an **adaptive budget allocation strategy**. This would involve dynamically adjusting the parameters P_ITER and R_ITER based on initial results. It might involve exploring other seeding strategies mentioned in Socrates I''s Archive or investigating hybrid approaches with non-local moves.


  So, I''m going to head to the Reflection Chamber and start planning this new project, focusing on an adaptive budget allocation for the MM-LP method. It feels like a natural extension of my current work.

  '
